    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d990">

                <pb facs="0899" n="891" sameAs="frus:frus1940v04/pg891"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.1163 Am 3/527</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 4867</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-07-23">July 23, 1940</date>.<lb/>[Received August
                        30.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to the Embassy’s despatch
                    No. 4740 dated June 3, 1940, transmitting a translation of a <hi
                        rendition="#italic">note verbale</hi> No. 106, American I, under date of May
                    24, 1940, from the Japanese Foreign Office<anchor n="2" xml:id="d990-f1"/>
                    replying to the Embassy’s representations regarding the bombing of the American
                    Catholic Mission at Linchwan, Kiangsi Province, as a result of Japanese aerial
                    bombardment on April 13, 1940,<anchor n="2a" xml:id="d990-f2"/> I have the honor
                    to transmit herewith a copy of the Embassy’s note No. 1590 dated July 23, 1940,
                    addressed to the Foreign Office<anchor n="3" xml:id="d990-f3"/> on this subject.
                    It will be observed that the note communicates additional data regarding this
                    inexcusable attack upon American property, and points out the failure of the
                    Japanese authorities in their report concerning the investigation of the bombing
                    attack to mention that the mission church, the most conspicuous object in
                    Linchwan, bore a large American flag on the roof, which the attacking aviators
                    could not have failed to observe in view of the fair weather which
                    prevailed.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Joseph</hi> C. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
